From cf7d3dd4b63dba12a68649b9c1532ef61d1e7cbb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 13:23:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix the NET PLAY panel: a method in the wrong constructor, an
 icon with no meta

Two separate reasons the button did nothing, both mine.

draw_netplay ended up as a static of RandomInput instead of GameInitBox.
The edit that added it was anchored on `static internal_draw = function()`
and there are several of those in the file - it landed in whichever
constructor came first, which is not the one that calls it. GML compiles
that happily and throws only when the branch actually draws:

    Variable GameInitBox.draw_netplay(...) not set before reading it

And the icon itself never drew, for an unrelated reason: spr_icon has 319
frames but global.sprite_meta.icon had 318 rows. gfx_draw_sprite_full
opens with `if (_m[0] == 0) { return; }` and gfx_meta hands back a zero
row for an index past the end of the table - so a frame with no meta row
is invisible, with no warning and no crash. Frame 318 now has its row,
32x32 to match the art.

Three checks so neither shape can hide again:

  check_static_scope   every unqualified call to a name that is a static
                       somewhere must resolve in this constructor or one it
                       inherits from. Run against the broken tree it names
                       the exact crash.
  check_sprite_meta    every sprite's frame count against its meta table.
  check_dup_statics    the PanelBar duplicate-handler check, which had not
                       made it into the repo yet.

And a correction to my own tooling, which is the part worth reading. All
three previously stripped comments with a regex, in the order block, line,
string. scr_game_init has

    #macro UPDATE_CHECK_URL "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/..."

and the // inside that string ate the rest of the line including its
closing quote, so every quote after it paired up wrong and whole
constructors dropped out of the checker's view. check_dup_statics said
"no duplicate statics" about a file it could not see, which is worse than
having no tool at all. tools/gml_scan.py is a real one-pass scanner that
decides by state instead of by pattern, with a self-test, and all three
use it. It also explains the brace imbalance I had been writing off as
pre-existing in scr_game_init: there was never one.

